# Mrs. Eliason's Blue Pen

Ben Reisinger sent the direct message at eleven-fifty-two on a Wednesday night in the second week of July, from the small square kitchen of a rented one-bedroom above a coffee roaster on Valencia Street.

The deal had cleared eight days earlier. His five-year-old company — a small quiet piece of AI infrastructure that seven hundred engineers you have not heard of use every morning at seven-forty when they open their laptops — had been acquired, on a Monday afternoon at three-oh-two Pacific, for a number the press release rendered in the plain corporate way that means, in the founder's account at Silicon Valley Bank the following Friday, one hundred and eighteen million dollars after tax.

He had not, in the eight days since, called anyone from home.

He had called his mother once, from the car, on the drive back from the closing dinner in Menlo Park. She had cried, and told him she was proud, and asked him whether he had eaten. He had said yes. He had eaten a small carton of pad see ew from the Thai place on 20th Street at eleven, standing at the sink.

On Wednesday night, in the small square kitchen, he opened his laptop. He typed *Eleanor Eliason* into the search bar of the alumni directory of the small central-Wisconsin K-12 district he had grown up in, which the district's foundation, in the modest budget of a small central-Wisconsin K-12 district, had put online in the fall of 2019 and had not updated since. He found her on the fourth page down, under *retired staff, 2019.* He copied her email into a plain window. He typed one line. He deleted it. He typed a paragraph. He deleted it. He typed two paragraphs. He read them twice.

He sent them at eleven-fifty-two.

## What Mrs. Eliason had in the green metal box

Eleanor Truelove Eliason has lived at 411 Elm Street in Berlin, Wisconsin, in a small buff-brick ranch with a screened-in side porch and a bird feeder her late husband Harold hung from the north eave in the April of 1994, since a warm Saturday in the June of 1978.

She taught ninth-grade English at Berlin High School on Memorial Drive from the September of 1982 until the last Friday of May, 2019. Thirty-seven Septembers. In each of them, in the second full week of October, on the same block of five class days, her ninth graders read *Death of a Salesman* aloud, out of the same green paperback classroom set the district had bought in the fall of 1987, and wrote, on the following Tuesday afternoon, a two-page essay on the sentence in the play that had struck them hardest.

She read every essay twice. She wrote her comments in ballpoint blue, in the small careful hand her own eleventh-grade English teacher, a woman named Sister Marie-Agnes at Assumption High in Wisconsin Rapids, had insisted on in the fall of 1974.

Of the roughly four thousand two hundred essays she read across thirty-seven years, she kept eleven.

She kept them, without any real system, in a small green metal Erie Insurance file box her father Norris had used, on the small oak rolltop in the back of the Delco Radio shop on West Huron Street, for the shop's warranty cards, from the September of 1962 until the shop closed on a Friday afternoon in the June of 1988. The box has been on the top shelf of the front-hall closet at 411 Elm Street since the second Saturday of July, 1988.

The seventh essay in the box, filed behind a small piece of yellow legal paper on which Mrs. Eliason had written, in her ballpoint blue, *Reisinger — Nov. 2005 — the loneliest one,* was Ben's.

He had been fifteen. He had written on Willy Loman's second-act line about the sample cases in the trunk. Mrs. Eliason had circled his final paragraph twice. In the top margin, in the same blue ballpoint, in the small careful hand, she had written the sentence Ben would, twenty-one years later, retype from memory in the small square kitchen on Valencia Street at eleven-fifty-one on a Wednesday night in July:

*There is a particular kind of loneliness in a man who cannot stop selling himself. Do not, please, become that man. — E.T.E.*

## What the university's spreadsheet said the same Wednesday

Somewhere on the second floor of a converted red-brick administration building on the campus of the small state university on the Fox River — the one Ben had enrolled in on a partial engineering scholarship in the September of 2010, and had left, quietly, after two semesters, in the spring of 2011, when a small Y Combinator acceptance email arrived on a Tuesday morning at nine-forty-one — the record for Benjamin K. Reisinger came up on the eighteenth row of the *lapsed / no contact* tab of the third-quarter alumni review spreadsheet.

*REISINGER, BENJAMIN K. — 411 N. Wisconsin St., Berlin WI 54923 (parental). Enrolled F10, withdrew S11 (in good standing). Major undeclared. No degree conferred. Lifetime giving $0. Wealth screen: NOT RUN (no degree, deprioritized). No engagement. Do not contact.*

The line had been on the *do not contact* tab, in one form or another, since the summer of 2011.

Nobody in the small three-person advancement office had, in the fifteen years since, opened Ben's file. Nobody had run his name through the wealth screen. Nobody had noticed the Series B TechCrunch line in the March of 2023, or the small quiet acquisition rumor on The Information in the second week of May, or the SEC filing in the last week of June that a paralegal in Palo Alto had filed on a Thursday afternoon at four-eleven.

Nobody had, in the fifteen years, thought to write Ben a plain warm note about Mrs. Eliason.

Nobody, honestly, had known there was a Mrs. Eliason.

## What Mrs. Eliason typed back at twelve-oh-four

Mrs. Eliason keeps her laptop, an eight-year-old Dell her son David set up for her the Saturday after Harold's funeral in the November of 2018, on the small maple secretary in the front room. She checks her email every morning at six-fifteen, with her first cup of coffee, and every evening at nine, before she goes upstairs.

On the Wednesday night, she had gone upstairs at ten past nine.

She came back down at eleven-forty, for a glass of water from the kitchen sink. She passed the secretary on her way back. The screen was dark, but she touched it without thinking. It woke up. She saw a new message from a name she had not, in twenty-one years, seen in her inbox.

She sat down in the small maple chair. She read the two paragraphs twice. She put her hand flat on the desk for a long moment.

Then she walked to the front hall, took the small green metal box down from the top shelf of the closet, brought it back to the secretary, and pulled the seventh essay out from behind the yellow legal paper.

The paperclip had rusted. The blue pen at the top was still bright.

She typed her reply at twelve-oh-four.

She did not tell Ben, in the reply, that she had kept the essay. She told him, in three plain paragraphs, that she remembered the November afternoon he had turned it in, and that his handwriting had been very small, and that she had been thinking, all these years, about the second-to-last sentence he had written about his father, which she was not going to repeat back to him now because he already knew it.

In the fourth paragraph, she wrote:

*If you are looking, Ben — and I think you are — for a small place to send some of what you have been given, there is a scholarship at the small state university on the Fox River, endowed in 1994 by a group of the retired English faculty for a first-generation student from a rural Wisconsin county whose essay in the freshman composition course reads, in the professor's own judgment, like the writer intends to keep telling the truth for a living. It is not a large scholarship. It is one thousand dollars a year. I have thought, for a long time, that it should be larger.*

She sent it at twelve-oh-nine.

She sat at the small maple secretary for another eight minutes, with her hand on the essay, in the quiet of the front room, before she went upstairs.

## What arrived on the second Wednesday of September

On the second Wednesday of September, at nine-fourteen in the morning, the director of the small state university's foundation — a fifty-eight-year-old former high-school journalism teacher named Karen Bergstrom Vandenheuvel, hired in the January of 2020 from the *Fond du Lac Reporter* — opened her email at the small oak desk in the corner office of the second-floor administration building on the Fox River.

The seventh message down was a wire notification from the university's bursar.

The line read *R-EN-DEPT-FUND-04: incoming wire, $4,200,000.00, memo — Eleanor T. Eliason English Scholarship, endowed. Origin: Reisinger, Benjamin K. Instructions attached.*

Karen read the line three times. Then she opened the attachment. Then she read the attachment twice. Then she got up from the desk and closed the door of the corner office, quietly, and sat back down, and cried, for about four minutes, at the small oak desk under the window.

The attachment was one page. It was signed by Ben. It named the scholarship, in Mrs. Eliason's full name, for a first-generation student from a rural Wisconsin county whose entering-year composition essay reads, in the professor's own judgment, like the writer intends to keep telling the truth for a living. It set the award, in the first year, at forty thousand dollars, renewable for four years, for six students.

At the bottom of the page, above Ben's signature, was one line.

*This one is because of Mrs. Eliason's blue pen.*

## The instrument this asks for

For twenty years, the category of software that sits between the small state university on the Fox River and Ben Reisinger has been sold, at increasingly expensive annual contracts, to answer one question: *of our forty thousand alumni, whose wealth-screen band changed this quarter?*

Ben's did not, until the second week of July, 2026. Ben's had, for fifteen years, been NOT RUN.

The tool answered the wrong question.

The question the tool would have needed to answer, on a Tuesday morning in the March of 2023, when the small Series B line first ran in TechCrunch, was: *of the four thousand two hundred essays this founder wrote in his life, is there a retired English teacher in Berlin, Wisconsin, who kept one of them?*

That is not a wealth screen. That is not a philanthropic-likelihood score. That is not a portfolio dashboard.

That is a small careful set of notes, assembled by someone who has spent a Tuesday morning listening — to the *Berlin Journal* retirement notice for E. T. Eliason from the June of 2019, to the small Delco Radio archive photograph the Berlin Historical Society ran in the November of 2007 of Norris Truelove behind the counter on West Huron in the summer of 1971, to the small paragraph on the third page of the *Berlin High School* yearbook of 2006 where a fifteen-year-old sophomore named Ben Reisinger, listed under *Fall Play Crew (props)*, thanked his ninth-grade English teacher, in the small yearbook-formal way of a shy boy who has never thanked anyone in print before, for *giving me back my father's voice at Christmas.*

The notes would have said: *When Ben's name changes color on the wealth-screen dashboard — and it will, probably in the summer of 2026 — do not send him a mail-merge in September. Send him, in the second week of March, a short handwritten note from the advancement office that mentions, by name, a ninth-grade English teacher named Eleanor Eliason and the November of 2005. Sign it. Mail it from Oshkosh. He will fold it into the small square kitchen drawer on Valencia Street beside his mother's Christmas card, and when the deal clears in July, he will already know where to write.*

That is what a tool can do.

That is the whole product.

## We are not the blue pen

Rōmy is not, and cannot be, the small careful hand of a sixty-seven-year-old retired ninth-grade English teacher writing *do not, please, become that man* in ballpoint blue on the top margin of a fifteen-year-old boy's essay on a rainy Tuesday afternoon in the November of 2005.

The blue pen is Mrs. Eliason's. The green metal Erie Insurance file box is Norris's, from the June of 1962. The essay is Ben's. The four-million-two-hundred-thousand-dollar wire transfer on the second Wednesday of September is between Ben and Mrs. Eliason and a small state university on the Fox River, and it will stay between them.

What a tool can do — quietly, on the second floor of the converted red-brick administration building on the Fox River, on a Monday morning in the March of 2023, twenty minutes before Karen Vandenheuvel opens the third-quarter portfolio spreadsheet — is hand her the small careful page a good listener would have written, if a good listener had been sitting on the small screened-in side porch at 411 Elm Street in Berlin, Wisconsin, on an August afternoon in 2022, watching Mrs. Eliason water the geraniums in the two hanging baskets her granddaughter Mira brought her for Mother's Day.

That is a smaller job than the industry has been trying to sell for twenty years.

It is also, with a great deal of tenderness, the whole job.

## A small assignment, with love

Sometime this week — a Saturday morning would be lovely — sit down at your own kitchen table with a plain notecard and a real pen.

Write to one former teacher.

Not the famous one. The ninth-grade one. The one whose blue pen — or red pen, or green pen, or the small pencil the geometry teacher used in the fall of your sophomore year — is still, somehow, in the top margin of a page in you that has never once been rewritten.

Tell her one sentence you remember. Tell her what you do now. Tell her thank you, in the plain warm way a person thanks another person who has quietly, for four decades, on eleven Novembers she never mentioned, been keeping a small green metal box on the top shelf of a front-hall closet in a small ranch on Elm Street.

Sign it.

Mail it from your kitchen table on Saturday morning, so the postmark reads the small town where you happen to live.

She is sixty-seven. Or seventy-one. Or seventy-eight. She is still checking her email at six-fifteen and at nine.

The essay is still in the box. ♡
